editorial:
## Reading the input correctly

`cin >> line` reads one whitespace-delimited token, so it would read only `A` from the sample. Use `getline`. This is the most common way to fail this problem before even starting it.

## Approach A: clean, then compare — O(n) time, O(n) space

Build a filtered copy and compare it with its reverse. Easy to get right.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string line;
    getline(cin, line);

    string clean;
    for (char c : line) {
        // isalnum and tolower take an INT and must not be passed a negative
        // value. A char can be signed, so cast through unsigned char first —
        // this is genuine undefined behaviour on non-ASCII bytes, not pedantry.
        unsigned char u = (unsigned char)c;
        if (isalnum(u)) clean += (char)tolower(u);
    }

    string reversed(clean.rbegin(), clean.rend());
    cout << (clean == reversed ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```

## Approach B: two pointers in place — O(n) time, O(1) space

Converge from both ends, skipping junk as you go.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s;
    getline(cin, s);

    int lo = 0, hi = (int)s.size() - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
        // Each skip loop must also respect lo < hi, or a string of pure
        // punctuation walks the pointers past each other.
        while (lo < hi && !isalnum((unsigned char)s[lo])) ++lo;
        while (lo < hi && !isalnum((unsigned char)s[hi])) --hi;
        if (tolower((unsigned char)s[lo]) != tolower((unsigned char)s[hi])) {
            cout << "false\n";
            return 0;
        }
        ++lo;
        --hi;
    }
    cout << "true\n";
}
```

## Why `lo < hi` inside the skip loops

On input `.,;` both skip loops would run off the end without that guard, and `s[lo]` would read out of bounds. The nested condition is not redundant — it is the bounds check.

## Pitfalls

- **Using `>>` instead of `getline`.**
- **Passing a possibly-negative `char` to `isalnum`/`tolower`.** Undefined behaviour; cast to `unsigned char`.
- **Forgetting that an empty or all-punctuation string is a palindrome.**
- **Mixing `>>` and `getline`** in a problem that has a count first: the newline left by `>>` makes the first `getline` return empty. Use `cin.ignore(...)` as in the I/O lesson.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Clean and compare | O(n) | O(n) |
| Two pointers | O(n) | O(1) |

