editorial:
## Sort first

Sorting costs O(n log n) — nothing, next to the O(n²) that follows — and buys two things: converging pointers become possible, and duplicates become adjacent so they are easy to skip.

## The algorithm — O(n^2) time

Fix the first element, then two-pointer the rest for a pair summing to its negation.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    long long count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i + 2 < n; ++i) {
        // Skip a duplicate FIRST element: it would regenerate every triplet
        // the previous identical value already produced.
        if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;

        // Sorted, so once the smallest candidate is positive nothing can
        // sum to zero. Cheap early exit.
        if (a[i] > 0) break;

        int lo = i + 1, hi = n - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            long long sum = a[i] + a[lo] + a[hi];
            if (sum < 0) {
                ++lo;
            } else if (sum > 0) {
                --hi;
            } else {
                ++count;
                // Advance BOTH past their duplicate runs, or the same
                // triplet is counted once per repeated value.
                int lo_value = a[lo], hi_value = a[hi];
                while (lo < hi && a[lo] == lo_value) ++lo;
                while (lo < hi && a[hi] == hi_value) --hi;
            }
        }
    }
    cout << count << '\n';
}
```

## The three duplicate skips

This problem is really a test of duplicate handling, and there are exactly three places it matters:

1. **The outer loop** — `if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i-1]) continue;`. Note `i > 0`, not `i >= 0`, or you skip the first element entirely.
2. **After a hit, advancing `lo`** past every copy of its value.
3. **After a hit, advancing `hi`** past every copy of its value.

On `0 0 0 0` the answer is 1. Without skip 1 you get 2; without skips 2 and 3 you loop on the same pair. Dry-run it.

## Why the pointer moves are safe

When `sum < 0`, no pair using the current `a[lo]` can reach zero, because `a[hi]` is already the largest value available. So `lo` can be discarded permanently. The mirror argument applies to `hi`. That is the correctness proof, and without it you have a guess — see the two-pointers lesson.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Three nested loops | O(n^3) | O(1) |
| Hash set per pair | O(n^2) | O(n) |
| Sort + two pointers | O(n^2) | O(1) extra |

At `n = 3000`, O(n³) is 2.7·10^10 — far too slow. O(n²) is 9·10^6, comfortable.

