editorial:
## Optimal — O(n) time, O(1) space

Start with the widest possible pair and move inwards, always advancing the pointer at the **shorter** line.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> h(n);
    for (long long& x : h) cin >> x;

    long long best = 0;
    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;

    while (lo < hi) {
        long long height = min(h[lo], h[hi]);
        long long width = hi - lo;
        best = max(best, height * width);

        // Move the SHORTER side. See the argument below.
        if (h[lo] < h[hi]) ++lo;
        else --hi;
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

## Why moving the shorter side is safe

This is the part worth understanding, because the algorithm is three lines and the proof is the whole content.

Suppose `h[lo] < h[hi]`. Consider every pair that uses `lo`. Each has width at most `hi - lo` — which we have just evaluated — and height at most `h[lo]`, since `lo` is the shorter line and the area is capped by the shorter side. So **no pair using `lo` can beat the one we just measured.** Position `lo` can be discarded permanently.

Had we moved the taller side instead, we would lose width while the height stays capped by the same short line, so the area can only shrink.

If the heights are equal, either move is safe by the same argument applied to both.

## Why the brute force fails

Every pair is O(n²) — at `n = 100000` that is 10^10 operations. Correct and far too slow.

## Pitfalls

- **Multiplying in `int`.** `width * height` can reach 10^5 · 10^4 = 10^9, which only just fits in a signed 32-bit int — and an intermediate in a larger expression may not. Use `long long`, as the constraints lesson advises.
- **Advancing the taller side**, or advancing both. Both give wrong answers on the sample.
- **Computing the height as the average or the sum.** It is the minimum: water spills over the lower line.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Every pair | O(n^2) | O(1) |
| Two pointers | O(n) | O(1) |

