editorial:
## The sliding window

Grow the window on the right. Whenever the new character breaks the no-repeat rule, shrink from the left until it holds again. Record the size at every step.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s;
    getline(cin, s);

    vector<int> count(256, 0);       // full byte range, so no char is unhandled
    int best = 0, left = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < (int)s.size(); ++right) {
        unsigned char c = (unsigned char)s[right];
        ++count[c];                                  // 1. grow

        // 2. shrink until the window is valid again. `left` never moves
        // backwards, so this inner loop runs at most n times IN TOTAL across
        // the whole outer loop — which is why this is O(n), not O(n^2).
        while (count[c] > 1) {
            --count[(unsigned char)s[left]];
            ++left;
        }

        best = max(best, right - left + 1);          // 3. record
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

## Why it is linear despite the nested loop

`left` only ever increases, and it is bounded by `n`. So the total number of `++left` executions across the entire run is at most `n`. Nested loops are not automatically quadratic — what matters is the total work, which is the same amortisation argument as `push_back`.

## The jump-the-pointer variant

Instead of stepping `left` one at a time, store the last index of each character and jump directly:

```cpp
vector<int> last(256, -1);
int best = 0, left = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < (int)s.size(); ++right) {
    unsigned char c = (unsigned char)s[right];
    // max() is essential: last[c] may be BEHIND left, from a character that
    // has already been shrunk out of the window. Without it, left moves
    // backwards and the window grows invalid.
    if (last[c] >= left) left = last[c] + 1;
    last[c] = right;
    best = max(best, right - left + 1);
}
```

The `>= left` guard is the subtle part. On `abba`, when the second `a` arrives, `last['a']` is 0 but `left` has already moved to 2 — jumping to `last['a'] + 1 = 1` would move `left` backwards and report 3 instead of 2.

## Dry run on `pwwkew`

| right | char | window | best |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | p | `p` | 1 |
| 1 | w | `pw` | 2 |
| 2 | w | shrink to `w` | 2 |
| 3 | k | `wk` | 2 |
| 4 | e | `wke` | 3 |
| 5 | w | shrink to `kew` | 3 |

Answer 3. Note the answer is `wke`, not the longer *subsequence* `pwke` — the substring must be contiguous.

## Pitfalls

- **`right - left`** instead of `right - left + 1` for an inclusive window.
- **Using `>>`** on input that may contain spaces.
- **An alphabet-26 table** when the input is arbitrary ASCII. Size it 256 and index through `unsigned char`.
- **Omitting the `>= left` guard** in the jumping variant.
- **Empty input.** Must print 0; the loop simply never runs.

## Complexity

O(n) time, O(k) space where `k` is the alphabet size — O(1) for a fixed alphabet.

