## Why a stack

At any point, the bracket that must close next is the most recently opened one that is still open. "Most recently added, accessed first" is the definition of a stack — so the structure is dictated by the problem rather than chosen.

## The solution — O(n) time, O(n) space

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s;
    getline(cin, s);

    // A table rather than a chain of ifs: shorter, and trivially extended
    // to more bracket kinds.
    const unordered_map<char, char> closes{{')', '('}, {']', '['}, {'}', '{'}};

    vector<char> open;                 // a vector used as a stack
    for (char c : s) {
        auto it = closes.find(c);
        if (it == closes.end()) {
            open.push_back(c);         // an opening bracket
        } else {
            // TWO distinct failures, and both must be checked:
            //   1. nothing is open at all   -> ")"
            //   2. the wrong thing is open  -> "(]"
            if (open.empty() || open.back() != it->second) {
                cout << "false\n";
                return 0;
            }
            open.pop_back();
        }
    }

    // 🛑 The check people forget. "((" survives every loop iteration and is
    // still unbalanced. Leftover opens mean invalid.
    cout << (open.empty() ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```

## The three failure modes

A correct solution must reject all three:

| Input | Why invalid | Caught by |
|---|---|---|
| `)` | Closes with nothing open | `open.empty()` |
| `(]` | Closes the wrong kind | `open.back() != expected` |
| `((` | Never closed | the final `open.empty()` |

Most wrong submissions handle the first two and omit the third.

## Why `([)]` fails

Trace it: push `(`, push `[`, then `)` arrives. The top of the stack is `[`, but `)` expects `(`. Mismatch, reject. The stack enforces *nesting* rather than mere counting — which is why counting brackets per kind is not a valid approach.

## The counting trap

For a single bracket kind you could just keep a counter, incrementing and decrementing and rejecting if it goes negative. With multiple kinds that fails: `([)]` has balanced counts for both kinds. You need the order, so you need the stack.

## Complexity

O(n) time; O(n) space in the worst case, which is `n` opening brackets in a row.

