## This is next-greater-element

Stripped of the story: for each position, find the next position holding a strictly larger value, and report the distance. The brute force scans right from every day — O(n²), too slow at 10^5.

## The monotonic stack

Keep a stack of **indices** whose temperatures are decreasing. When a warmer day arrives, it is the answer for every index on the stack that is cooler — so pop them and record.

The insight: **you resolve an element at the moment you pop it**, not when you push it.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> t(n);
    for (int& x : t) cin >> x;

    vector<int> wait(n, 0);        // 0 is already the "never warmer" answer
    vector<int> stack;             // INDICES, with decreasing temperatures

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        // Strictly warmer, so `<` not `<=`. With `<=`, an equal temperature
        // would wrongly count as warmer.
        while (!stack.empty() && t[stack.back()] < t[i]) {
            wait[stack.back()] = i - stack.back();   // the distance
            stack.pop_back();
        }
        stack.push_back(i);
    }
    // Whatever is left never found a warmer day; already 0.

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cout << wait[i] << " \n"[i == n - 1];
}
```

## Why indices and not temperatures

The answer is a *distance*, so you need the position. Pushing temperatures loses it. This is the standard mistake with monotonic stacks and the reason the lesson insists on it.

## Why it is O(n)

Each index is pushed exactly once and popped at most once, so the total number of stack operations is at most `2n`. The `while` inside the `for` does not make it quadratic — again, total work is what matters.

## Dry run on the sample

| i | t[i] | pops (index -> wait) | stack after |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 73 | — | [0] |
| 1 | 74 | 0 -> 1 | [1] |
| 2 | 75 | 1 -> 1 | [2] |
| 3 | 71 | — | [2,3] |
| 4 | 69 | — | [2,3,4] |
| 5 | 72 | 4 -> 1, 3 -> 2 | [2,5] |
| 6 | 76 | 5 -> 1, 2 -> 4 | [6] |
| 7 | 73 | — | [6,7] |

Indices 6 and 7 remain and stay 0. Result: `1 1 4 2 1 1 0 0`.

## Pitfalls

- **Pushing values instead of indices.**
- **`<=` instead of `<`** when the problem says *strictly* warmer.
- **Forgetting that unresolved entries are 0** — initialise the output array to 0 and there is nothing to clean up.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Brute force | O(n^2) | O(1) |
| Monotonic stack | O(n) | O(n) |

