editorial:
## Write it with half-open bounds

The single best habit for binary search is to use `[lo, hi)` with `hi` one past the end. The loop condition, the updates and the empty case then all follow mechanically.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    long long target;
    cin >> n >> target;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    int lo = 0, hi = n;                 // hi is EXCLUSIVE
    int found = -1;

    while (lo < hi) {
        // lo + (hi - lo) / 2, never (lo + hi) / 2: the latter can overflow
        // when both are large. Harmless at n = 10^5, a real bug when the
        // bounds are values rather than indices.
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;

        if (a[mid] == target) {
            found = mid;
            break;
        }
        if (a[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;               // mid is ruled out, hence +1
        } else {
            hi = mid;                   // mid is ruled out, and hi is
                                        // exclusive, so no -1
        }
    }
    cout << found << '\n';
}
```

## The asymmetry is the point

`lo = mid + 1` but `hi = mid`. Both exclude `mid`, which has just been tested — but because `hi` is exclusive, excluding `mid` means *setting* `hi` to it. Writing `lo = mid` instead of `mid + 1` makes no progress when `mid == lo`, and the loop never ends. Dry-run `[1, 3]` searching for 2 to see it.

## In a contest, use the library

```cpp
auto it = lower_bound(a.begin(), a.end(), target);
cout << ((it != a.end() && *it == target) ? it - a.begin() : -1) << '\n';
```

`lower_bound` gives the first element **>= target**, so the `*it == target` check is what distinguishes "found" from "would be inserted here". Omitting it is a common bug. Write the manual loop only when the predicate is custom — which is exactly the case for binary search on the answer.

## Invariant

State it and the code writes itself: **if the target is present, its index is in `[lo, hi)`.** Every branch must preserve that, and the loop ends when the range is empty.

## Complexity

O(log n) time, O(1) space. At `n = 10^5` that is 17 comparisons.

