editorial:
## The key observation

Split a rotated sorted array at any midpoint and **at least one of the two halves is properly sorted.** Identify which, then check whether the target lies inside it. If it does, search there; otherwise search the other half.

That is still one comparison per step, so the complexity stays O(log n) even though the array is not sorted.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    long long target;
    cin >> n >> target;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    // Closed bounds [lo, hi] here, because the comparisons reference a[hi].
    // Pick a convention per function and stay inside it.
    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1, found = -1;

    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) {
            found = mid;
            break;
        }

        if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {
            // Left half [lo, mid] is sorted. `<=` not `<`, so that lo == mid
            // (a one-element half) is treated as sorted rather than falling
            // into the else branch.
            if (a[lo] <= target && target < a[mid]) hi = mid - 1;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            // Right half [mid, hi] is sorted.
            if (a[mid] < target && target <= a[hi]) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    cout << found << '\n';
}
```

## Why `a[lo] <= a[mid]` identifies a sorted left half

If the left half contained the rotation point, its first element would be larger than its last — so `a[lo] > a[mid]`. The absence of that inversion means no rotation point inside, hence sorted. The `<=` handles `lo == mid`, where the half is a single element and trivially sorted; using `<` sends that case to the wrong branch.

## The range checks

Once you know a half is sorted, deciding whether the target is in it is a plain two-sided comparison — and the bounds must be *inclusive on the sorted end and exclusive at mid*, because `mid` has already been tested and rejected. Getting `<` and `<=` mixed up here is where this problem is usually lost.

## Dry run: target 0 in `4 5 6 7 0 1 2`

- `lo=0 hi=6 mid=3`, `a[3]=7`. `a[0]=4 <= 7`, so left `[4,5,6,7]` is sorted. Is `4 <= 0 < 7`? No. So `lo=4`.
- `lo=4 hi=6 mid=5`, `a[5]=1`. `a[4]=0 <= 1`, left `[0,1]` sorted. Is `0 <= 0 < 1`? Yes. So `hi=4`.
- `lo=4 hi=4 mid=4`, `a[4]=0` equals target. Return 4.

## A note on duplicates

With duplicate values this approach breaks down: `a[lo] == a[mid]` no longer tells you which half is sorted, and the worst case degrades to O(n). The constraints here guarantee distinct values; the duplicate variant is a separate, harder problem.

## Complexity

O(log n) time, O(1) space.

