editorial:
## The idea

The minimum is the rotation point — the only place where an element is smaller than its predecessor. Binary search for it by comparing the midpoint against the **last** element.

- If `a[mid] > a[hi]`, the rotation point is strictly to the right of `mid`. So `lo = mid + 1`.
- Otherwise `a[mid] <= a[hi]`, meaning `[mid, hi]` is sorted, so the minimum is at `mid` or to its left. So `hi = mid`.

The loop narrows until `lo == hi`, which is the answer.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] > a[hi]) {
            // mid cannot be the minimum — something to its right is smaller.
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            // mid MIGHT be the minimum, so it stays in the range. Writing
            // hi = mid - 1 here would discard the answer.
            hi = mid;
        }
    }
    cout << a[lo] << '\n';
}
```

## Why compare against `a[hi]` and not `a[lo]`

Comparing against `a[lo]` does not work cleanly: in an unrotated array `a[mid] > a[lo]` is true, which would send you rightwards and away from the minimum at index 0. Comparing against `a[hi]` handles the unrotated case naturally — `a[mid] <= a[hi]` is always true there, so `hi` marches down to 0.

That means **no special case is needed for a zero rotation**, which is the usual source of a wrong answer here.

## Why `hi = mid` and not `mid - 1`

In the `else` branch, `mid` is still a candidate. Excluding it can discard the answer — for example on `[2, 1]`: `lo=0, hi=1, mid=0`, `a[0]=2 > a[1]=1`, so `lo=1`, loop ends, answer `a[1]=1`. Correct. But on `[1, 2]`: `mid=0`, `a[0]=1 <= a[1]=2`, so `hi=0`, loop ends, answer `a[0]=1`. Had we written `hi = mid - 1` we would get `hi = -1` and read out of bounds.

## Dry run on `3 4 5 1 2`

| lo | hi | mid | a[mid] vs a[hi] | action |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 4 | 2 | 5 > 2 | lo = 3 |
| 3 | 4 | 3 | 1 <= 2 | hi = 3 |
| 3 | 3 | — | — | stop, a[3] = 1 |

## Complexity

O(log n) time, O(1) space. The O(n) `min_element` scan is of course correct, and is what to write if the log solution is not coming — but the problem specifically asks for logarithmic.
